title: Connection to the daemon
tags: GUI
kind: reference
aliases: connection daemon-connection banner unreachable incompatible api-version diagnostics
summary: Which daemon the GUI talks to, the banners when it cannot or should not, and how the daemon's warnings reach the message log.

The GUI talks to a daemon it does not start: on `127.0.0.1`, at `daemon-port` of
[[GUI configuration]] (default 7523, the daemon's own default, so a fresh install
connects without any flag), or at `--daemon-port` given at launch.
`daemon:set url <url>` points it elsewhere for the rest of the session. Every request carries the
daemon's session token ([[Session tokens]]).

Everything goes through the GUI's Rust side — the web view cannot reach the daemon
itself — and the panels read nothing from a cache: each read is a request.

!! Health and the banners

Every five seconds (`daemon-health-poll-secs`), and at once after `daemon:set`, the
GUI asks `GET /health` ([[Health and diagnostics]]).

* ''Daemon unreachable'' — no answer, or not a 200: a red banner says so above the tabs. The GUI itself keeps working — panels, command input, keys, the scripting API — but whatever needs the daemon fails, with its error, until it answers again. Nothing is disabled beforehand.
* ''Incompatible daemon'' — it answers, but reports an `api_version` other than the GUI's own, or none: the banner says that data may be missing or wrong, and that both should be rebuilt from the same source. It is the usual mishap of development — a rebuilt daemon restarted under a GUI still running the old code, or the reverse — which otherwise shows as empty or broken panels without a word.

The panels never pass a failed read off as an empty result: `metarecord-list` and
`file-manager` show the error in their body, and stay there, rather than an empty
list; `file-manager` falls back to browsing the disk alone rather than hanging on
''Loading…'' when the daemon does not answer.

!! The daemon's warnings

The daemon is its own process, and the GUI cannot read its standard error. On each
health poll that reaches it, the GUI reads the daemon's diagnostics feed
(`GET /diagnostics`) and appends each new line to the message logs, as
`daemon <scope>: <message>` — `error:` spelled out for an error, a warning being the ordinary case.
The `info` level arrives too: it is how each watcher flush says what it applied
([[What a flush reports]]) — the answer to ''why did something just happen when I
did nothing?''. When the feed lost entries before they were read, a line says so.

A line about a repository goes to the workspaces on that repository only; a line
about none, to every workspace; a line about a repository no workspace shows, to
every workspace too ([[Why the daemon's warnings are routed by repository]]).

The GUI starts reading from the beginning of the feed, so the first poll brings what
the daemon said before the GUI was up — why a repository failed to load, say. It
moves on only after a well-formed answer: a daemon out of reach, or an answer of an
unexpected shape, is read again from the same point, so nothing is skipped.

